# EXPECT: which the importing program also uses
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE is the EXEMPTION arm of the import-collision guard,
# where all three defects found in this guard have lived and where the refusal
# tier had no coverage at all when it was written.
#
# The exemption lets `import linalg` + `import complex` through: linalg binds
# `complex` too, but it binds it VIA `import complex`, so both names denote the
# same spliced module dict and there is no collision. Two earlier versions got
# the condition wrong:
#
#   v1  tested a global set AFTER the recursive splice had registered the
#       module's own transitive imports, so it exempted names the program never
#       imported: `complex is 7` + `import linalg` printed the module dict.
#   v2  tested only the PROGRAM's imports — by NAME rather than by KIND. It
#       proves the program imports something called `bn`, not that the module's
#       `bn` is the same thing. The real stdlib reaches it: lib/list.eigs binds
#       `map` with a `define`, so `import map` + `import list` sailed through.
#
# This is the v2 shape, minimised, and it was a SILENT WRONG ANSWER — build
# rc=0, zero-byte log, VM 5 against AOT 99.
#
# The exemption now requires BOTH sides to bind the name via `import`, which is
# the one case where they provably denote the same dict.

import _kind_my
import _kind_mx
print of _kind_my.val
